/*
 * The Examples page, and the scripts in examples/ registered for it, with the
 * formatted description shown above each script's source. Doxygen does not
 * read comments in the scripts themselves, so keep each block here in step
 * with that script's header comment, with the list on the Examples page, with
 * the scripts under Examples in DoxygenLayout.xml (the side menu) and with the
 * Examples table in README.md.
 *
 * The Examples page replaces Doxygen's own list (hidden in DoxygenLayout.xml),
 * which can only show file paths such as 01_basics/read_analog_voltage/main.py. For the same
 * reason each script's page title is set by the hidden example-title span at
 * the start of its block, which a script in footer.html moves into the title.
 */

/**
@page example_scripts Examples

Example scripts for the Phyduino Pico, in folders named after the Arduino
IDE's example categories. Each example is a `main.py` in its own folder, because
MicroPython runs `main.py` each time the board starts. Each page below explains
what the script does, how to wire it and what it prints, followed by its source.

Copy `phyduino.py` to the board first (the Phyduino Pico firmware has it built
in), then run a script from VS Code (MicroPico's **Run**), PyCharm, or with
`mpremote run examples/<folder>/<name>/main.py`. To have it run every time the
board starts, copy its `main.py` to the board:
`mpremote cp examples/<folder>/<name>/main.py :`, then press RESET.

Every script has the same shape as an Arduino sketch: setup() runs once, then
loop() runs over and over. The main() at the end of each file starts them; leave
it as it is.

@par 01_basics
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref 01_basics/bare_minimum/main.py "Bare Minimum" | The empty setup() and loop() to start a program from |
| @ref 01_basics/blink/main.py "Blink" | Blinking the on-board LED |
| @ref 01_basics/analog_read_serial/main.py "Analog Read Serial" | All six analogue pins as 12-bit codes |
| @ref 01_basics/digital_read_serial/main.py "Digital Read Serial" | Printing the state of a pushbutton |
| @ref 01_basics/fade/main.py "Fade" | Fading the on-board LED with PWM |
| @ref 01_basics/read_analog_voltage/main.py "Read Analog Voltage" | All six analogue pins in millivolts |

@par 02_digital
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref 02_digital/blink_without_delay/main.py "Blink Without Delay" | Timing with time.ticks_ms() instead of time.sleep() |
| @ref 02_digital/button/main.py "Button" | Lighting the LED while a pushbutton is pressed |
| @ref 02_digital/debounce/main.py "Debounce" | Ignoring contact bounce so each press counts once |
| @ref 02_digital/digital_input_pullup/main.py "Digital Input Pullup" | A pushbutton with the pin's own pull-up resistor |
| @ref 02_digital/state_change_detection/main.py "State Change Detection" | Counting presses by detecting the edge |
| @ref 02_digital/tone_melody/main.py "Tone Melody" | Playing a tune on a piezo buzzer |
| @ref 02_digital/tone_pitch_follower/main.py "Tone Pitch Follower" | A pitch that follows an analogue input |

@par 03_analog
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref 03_analog/analog_in_out_serial/main.py "Analog In Out Serial" | Setting LED brightness from a potentiometer |
| @ref 03_analog/calibration/main.py "Calibration" | Learning a sensor's range at start-up |
| @ref 03_analog/smoothing/main.py "Smoothing" | A running average of the last 10 readings |

@par 04_communication
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref 04_communication/ascii_table/main.py "ASCII Table" | Characters with their decimal, hex, octal and binary values |
| @ref 04_communication/graph/main.py "Graph" | Sending readings to a serial plotter |
| @ref 04_communication/i2c_scanner/main.py "I2C Scanner" | Finding the devices on the I2C bus |
| @ref 04_communication/physical_pixel/main.py "Physical Pixel" | Switching the LED from the computer |
| @ref 04_communication/uart_loopback/main.py "UART Loopback" | Sending and receiving on UART0 |

@par phyduino_pico
Features of this board that the Arduino examples have no match for.
| Example | What it shows |
|---|---|
| @ref phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py "Analog Averaging" | How the averaging setting steadies a reading |
| @ref phyduino_pico/analog_differential/main.py "Analog Differential" | The voltage between two pins |
| @ref phyduino_pico/vbus_monitor/main.py "VBUS Monitor" | The USB-C supply voltage and the CPU temperature |
| @ref phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py "USB-PD Power" | Asking a USB-PD charger for a higher voltage |
*/

/**
 * @example 01_basics/blink/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Blink example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Blinks the on-board green LED once a second.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * `led`: Led.toggle()
 */

/**
 * @example 01_basics/analog_read_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Read Serial example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads all six analogue pins as 12-bit codes (0-4095), twice a second.
 *
 * A0-A2: 0 = 0 V, 4095 = 3.3 V (RP2040 ADC). A3-A5: 0 = 0 V, 4095 = 2.048 V
 * (on-board MAX11635 ADC).
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a potentiometer wiper (ends on GND and 3.3 V) to any pin to see it
 * change; keep A3-A5 at or below 2.048 V. Unconnected pins drift.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A0=2048  A1=   3  A2=   0  A3=2048  A4=   0  A5=   0
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example 01_basics/read_analog_voltage/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Read Analog Voltage example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the voltage on every analogue pin, in millivolts, twice a second.
 *
 * The library scales each pin with its own full scale (3.3 V for A0-A2,
 * 2.048 V for A3-A5), so the numbers can be compared directly.
 *
 * @par Output
 * A0 to A5 in order:
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * 1650.0 2.4 0.0 1024.0 0.0 0.0
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Averaging example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Shows how averaging steadies a reading. For each setting, takes 100
 * readings of A0 and A3 and prints the lowest, the highest and the spread
 * between them (in ADC steps), then waits 5 seconds and starts again.
 *
 * analog_averaging() applies to every analogue pin: A3-A5 average in the
 * external ADC itself, A0-A2 take the samples one after another.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a steady voltage, e.g. a potentiometer wiper, to A0 (0-3.3 V) and
 * A3 (0-2.048 V), or leave them as they are.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * averaging  1  A0  lowest 2040  highest 2056  spread  16
 * averaging  1  A3  lowest 2046  highest 2050  spread   4
 * ...
 * averaging 32  A0  lowest 2047  highest 2049  spread   2
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino_pico/analog_differential/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog Differential example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Measures the voltage between two analogue pins, e.g. across a resistor or a
 * sensor bridge, twice a second.
 *
 * - **A3 - A4**: true differential. The on-board ADC samples both pins at the
 *   same instant, so noise common to both cancels. Range ±1024 mV in 0.5 mV
 *   steps (a larger difference is measured as two separate readings).
 * - **A0 - A1**: computed from readings taken one after the other. Fine for
 *   slow signals, but noise on both pins does not cancel.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Keep every pin between 0 V and its full scale (A0-A2: 3.3 V, A3-A5: 2.048 V).
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * A3 - A4:   100.0 mV    A0 - A1:    99.1 mV
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino_pico/vbus_monitor/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>VBUS Monitor example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the USB-C supply voltage (VBUS) and the RP2040's temperature once a
 * second. VBUS is about 5 V from a computer; a USB-PD charger can raise it
 * (see @ref phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py "USB-PD Power"). VBUS is also on the header's VIN pin.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * VBUS 5.02 V    CPU 24.3 C
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example phyduino_pico/usb_pd_power/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>USB-PD Power example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Asks a USB-PD charger for up to `MAX_VOLTS`, then prints the board's
 * profiles, the contract and the measured VBUS once a second.
 *
 * A charger takes the only USB-C port, so there is no USB connection to the
 * computer while it is plugged in. Copy this `main.py` to the board so it runs
 * by itself, and watch the output on UART0 (TX on D1, 115200 baud)
 * with a USB-serial adapter. The green POK2 LED lights at 9 V, POK3 at 20 V.
 *
 * @warning VBUS also feeds the VIN pin, so VIN follows the contract:
 * disconnect anything on VIN that can't take the voltage you ask for.
 *
 * The request lasts until the board loses power.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Profiles: 5 V 2 A, 9 V 1 A
 * Contract: offer 2, 1.00 A    VBUS 9.01 V
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example 04_communication/i2c_scanner/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>I2C Scanner example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Lists the addresses of the I2C devices on SDA (D18) and SCL (D19), every
 * 5 seconds, so a module can be plugged in while it runs.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * The board has no pull-up resistors on these pins. Most sensor modules
 * include them; otherwise fit 4.7 kOhm resistors from SDA and SCL to 3.3 V.
 * The on-board USB-PD controller is on a separate bus and does not appear.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * Found: 0x3C, 0x76
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example 04_communication/uart_loopback/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>UART Loopback example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Sends a numbered message out of UART0 and reads it back, twice a second.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * Connect a jumper wire from D1 (TX) to D0 (RX) first; without it, nothing
 * comes back.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sent 'hello 0', received 'hello 0'
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example 01_basics/bare_minimum/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Bare Minimum example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * The smallest program: set-up code goes in setup(), which runs once, and the
 * main code goes in loop(), which runs over and over. Copy this file to start
 * a new program.
 */

/**
 * @example 01_basics/digital_read_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Digital Read Serial example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the state of a pushbutton on D2, 1 when pressed and 0 when not, ten
 * times a second.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V. The pin's pull-down resistor holds D2 at
 * 0 while the button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example 01_basics/fade/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Fade example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Fades the on-board LED up and down with PWM, about once every three
 * seconds. The duty cycle goes from 0 (off) to 65535 (fully on) in 51 steps,
 * 30 ms apart, then back down.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * pwm()
 */

/**
 * @example 02_digital/blink_without_delay/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Blink Without Delay example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Blinks the on-board LED once a second without time.sleep(), so loop() is
 * free to do other work between changes. Each pass checks how long it has
 * been since the last change with `time.ticks_ms()` and `time.ticks_diff()`.
 */

/**
 * @example 02_digital/button/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Button example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Lights the on-board LED while a pushbutton on D2 is pressed.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V. The pin's pull-down resistor holds D2 at
 * 0 while the button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example 02_digital/debounce/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Debounce example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Toggles the on-board LED each time a pushbutton on D2 is pressed, ignoring
 * the contact bounce that makes one press look like several. A new reading
 * only counts once it has stayed the same for `DEBOUNCE_MS`.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V; the pull-down holds D2 at 0 while the
 * button is open.
 */

/**
 * @example 02_digital/digital_input_pullup/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Digital Input Pullup example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads a pushbutton on D2 using the pin's pull-up resistor, so no other
 * parts are needed, prints its state and lights the on-board LED while it is
 * pressed.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and GND. The pull-up holds D2 at 1 while the button
 * is open, so the logic is inverted: pressed reads 0.
 */

/**
 * @example 02_digital/state_change_detection/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>State Change Detection example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Counts presses of a pushbutton on D2 and lights the on-board LED on every
 * fourth press. It acts on the change from released to pressed (an edge), not
 * on the button being held down.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A pushbutton between D2 and 3.3 V; the pull-down holds D2 at 0 while the
 * button is open.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * on
 * number of button pushes: 1
 * off
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example 02_digital/tone_melody/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Tone Melody example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Plays a short tune on a piezo buzzer on D8, then waits two seconds and
 * plays it again. MicroPython has no `tone()`: a PWM output at 50 % duty
 * makes the note, and a duty of 0 is silence.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A piezo buzzer between D8 and GND (or a small speaker in series with a
 * 100 Ohm resistor).
 *
 * @par Uses
 * pwm()
 */

/**
 * @example 02_digital/tone_pitch_follower/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Tone Pitch Follower example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Plays a pitch on a piezo buzzer on D8 that follows the voltage on A0, from
 * 120 Hz at 0 V to 1500 Hz at 3.3 V, and prints the reading and the pitch.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A piezo buzzer between D8 and GND, and a potentiometer wiper (ends on GND
 * and 3.3 V) or a light sensor divider on A0.
 *
 * @par Uses
 * pwm(), analog_read()
 */

/**
 * @example 03_analog/analog_in_out_serial/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Analog In Out Serial example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Reads a potentiometer on A0, sets the on-board LED's brightness from it
 * with PWM, and prints both numbers.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper on A0, with its ends on GND and 3.3 V.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * sensor = 2048   output = 32775
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example 03_analog/calibration/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Calibration example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Learns the range of a sensor on A0 during the first five seconds, then sets
 * the on-board LED's brightness from where each reading falls in that range.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A sensor, e.g. a light-dependent resistor divider, on A0. While the LED is
 * on at the start, give the sensor its lowest and highest values, e.g. cover
 * it and then shine a light on it.
 */

/**
 * @example 03_analog/smoothing/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Smoothing example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints a running average of the last 10 readings of A0, which steadies a
 * noisy signal. Each new reading replaces the oldest one. For averaging done
 * by the board itself, see @ref phyduino_pico/analog_averaging/main.py "Analog Averaging".
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper or sensor on A0 (0-3.3 V).
 */

/**
 * @example 04_communication/ascii_table/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>ASCII Table example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the printable ASCII characters, 33 ("!") to 126 ("~"), each with its
 * value in decimal, hexadecimal, octal and binary.
 *
 * @par Output
 * @code{.unparsed}
 * ASCII Table ~ Character Map
 * !, dec: 33, hex: 21, oct: 41, bin: 100001
 * ", dec: 34, hex: 22, oct: 42, bin: 100010
 * ...
 * @endcode
 */

/**
 * @example 04_communication/graph/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Graph example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Prints the reading on A0, one number per line, 50 times a second, for a
 * serial plotter to draw as a graph. Close MicroPico's terminal first so the
 * port is free.
 *
 * @par Wiring
 * A potentiometer wiper or sensor on A0 (0-3.3 V).
 */

/**
 * @example 04_communication/physical_pixel/main.py
 * @htmlonly<span class="example-title" hidden>Physical Pixel example script</span>@endhtmlonly
 * Turns the on-board LED on when it receives "H" and off when it receives "L"
 * from the computer. Type the letters in the REPL terminal (MicroPico's
 * terminal or mpremote) while the script runs. `sys.stdin.read(1)` waits for
 * the next character, so loop() pauses until one arrives.
 */
